/**
@defgroup errors the error values of the library and the limits of a build

A call that reads CBOR gives a `std::expected` with a `cbor::error` when
it fails. Bad input never throws. The `cbor::error` names the fault.
Include `<cbor/cbor.hpp>`.

A `cbor::error` converts to a `std::error_code` of the category
`cbor::category()`. The message of that `std::error_code` is the text of
the fault.

A limit of the build stops input that holds a string or a container that
is too large. A macro sets each limit. `cbor::limits` gives its value.

@section errors_values THE ERROR VALUES

Each value is one enumerator of `cbor::error`.

- `too_little_data`: the bytes end before the item ends.
- `syntax_error`: a head is not well-formed, or a simple value is reserved in the two-byte form.
  A call that reads exactly one item finds bytes after it: `cbor::diagnostic_notation` and the
  content of a tag 24.
- `indefinite_length`: a head uses indefinite length, and the call does not read it.
- `nesting_depth_exceeded`: a reader that recurses reaches the end of the stack of its thread (`cbor::limits`).
- `inadmissible_type_for_tag_content`: a tag has content that its definition does not allow.
- `sharedref_index_not_marked`: a tag 29 names no tag 28 before it.
- `sharedref_not_complete`: `cbor::structs` %decode meets a tag 29 that names a tag 28 in a skipped member.
- `sharedref_not_defined`: `cbor::decode` meets a tag 29 that names a tag 28 whose content is only tags 28 and this tag 29.
- `reserved_simple_value`: an encode meets a simple value from 24 to 31.
- `unsupported_value`: the encoder meets a typed array with a reserved tag, or a tag outside RFC 8746; `cbor::encode()` writes a tag 29 only with `cbor::cycles`.
- `not_indexable`: a key or an index meets an item that is not a map or an array.
- `index_out_of_bounds`: the index is outside the array.
- `key_not_found`: the key is not in the map.
- `invalid_path`: the text of a %path is not valid.
- `incorrect_type`: a typed read finds another kind of item than the type `T` takes.
- `number_out_of_range`: the number does not fit `T`.
- `cyclic_data_structure`: the library does not return it. A writer of the program can return it for a cycle.
- `unpopulated_table_index`: a reference in a `cbor::schema<T>` message names an entry that its table does not have.
- `nodelist_too_long`: one step of a %path selects more nodes than the item has bytes.
- `duplicate_key`: a deterministic check finds a key twice in one map.
- `no_buffer_space`: the writer of an encode gives this error.
- `value_too_large`: the writer of an encode gives this error.
- `not_enough_memory`: the writer of an encode gives this error. A refused allocation in `cbor::structs` or `cbor::schema` %decode gives it too.
- `io_error`: the writer of an encode gives this error.
- `string_length_exceeded`: a string is longer than `cbor::limits::string_length`.
- `container_elements_exceeded`: a container has more items than `cbor::limits::container_elements`.
- `not_singular_query`: `cbor::path::compile<T>` gets a %path that is not singular, and `T` is not `nodelist`.
- `amplification_limit_breach`: `cbor::encode()` with `cbor::cycles` would write more than `cbor::limits::maximum_amplification` times the size of the tree, past `cbor::limits::amplification_activation_threshold`.

@section errors_conversion THE CONVERSION TO std::error_code

`std::is_error_code_enum` is true for `cbor::error`. So a `cbor::error`
converts to a `std::error_code` with no cast. `cbor::make_error_code()`
makes the same value. A program seldom calls it by name.

A `std::error_code` compares equal to a `cbor::error` with `==`.

The category is `cbor::category()`. Its name is `cbor`. Each call gives
the same object. Its `message()` gives the text of an error. A value that
is no `cbor::error` gives `unknown cbor error`.

Four values of `cbor::error` compare equal to a `std::errc`:`no_buffer_space`,
`value_too_large`, `not_enough_memory` and `io_error`. Each one compares
equal to the `std::errc` of the same name. No other error compares equal
to a `std::errc`.

A `cbor::error` and a `std::errc` do not compare directly. The direct
comparison does not compile. Put the `std::error_code` on the left.

@section errors_wrong_use WRONG USE

A wrong use of the library does not compile where the compiler can see
it. For example, a temporary `std::string` as the bytes of a view does
not compile.

Where the compiler cannot see a wrong use, the library throws
`std::logic_error`. For example:

- an empty owner, or a null `std::shared_ptr<std::string const>`;
- a `cbor::lazy{}` that holds no item.

`std::logic_error` is a fault of the program, not of the input.

@section errors_limits THE LIMITS OF A BUILD

Each limit is a `static constexpr` member of `cbor::limits`. No call
reads a limit at run time.

- `CBOR_STRING_LENGTH_DEFAULT` sets `cbor::limits::string_length`. It counts the bytes of one text or byte string. Its error is `string_length_exceeded`.
- `CBOR_CONTAINER_ELEMENTS_DEFAULT` sets `cbor::limits::container_elements`. It counts the elements of one array, or the pairs of one map. Its error is `container_elements_exceeded`.
- `CBOR_AMPLIFICATION_ACTIVATION_THRESHOLD_DEFAULT` sets `cbor::limits::amplification_activation_threshold`. Its default is 8388608 bytes (8 MiB). Its error is `amplification_limit_breach`.
- `CBOR_MAXIMUM_AMPLIFICATION_DEFAULT` sets `cbor::limits::maximum_amplification`. Its default is 100. Its error is `amplification_limit_breach`.

A macro that the build does not set takes its default. The default of the
first two limits is `SIZE_MAX`. A limit at `SIZE_MAX` is not set, and its
check is not in the program. Every translation unit of one program uses
the same values. A value that does not fit `std::size_t` does not
compile.

`cbor::encode()` with `cbor::cycles` gives `amplification_limit_breach`,
and writes nothing, when the output would be larger than
`cbor::limits::amplification_activation_threshold` and than
`cbor::limits::maximum_amplification` times the tree.

A limit counts what a call reads or decodes. An item that a call only
skips does not count. The error comes at the read that meets the value.
A count in the input reserves no memory. The library does not bound the
size of the input. The program bounds it before it calls the library.

Nesting has no fixed limit. A reader that recurses stops where the stack
of its thread ends. A call that comes within 16 KiB of the end of the
stack gives `nesting_depth_exceeded`. On Windows, and with a sanitizer,
the distance is 32 KiB.

Each thread reads the bounds of its own stack once, at its first check.
On Linux (glibc and musl), macOS and Windows, the bounds come from the
system. On WebAssembly without threads, and on picolibc, they come from
the linker. Another target does not compile.

A frame outside these bounds, such as the stack of a fiber or of a
signal handler, is not checked. The first check of a thread does not run
in a signal handler. On Linux, a change of `RLIMIT_STACK` after the first
check is outside the bound. So is a mapping that a program places with
`MAP_FIXED` below the stack of the main thread. On picolibc, a stack
smaller than the reserve refuses every nested item.

`cbor::item_size()`, `cbor::sequence_reader` and a %path of child
segments do not recurse, so they read any depth. A deep value of a user
type that `cbor::structs` or `cbor::schema` reads is destroyed and copied
by its own recursion. A thread with a smaller stack than the one that
decoded the value can overflow there.

@manonly
.SH "EXAMPLES"
@endmanonly
@snippet doc_reference_errors.cpp errors group
*/
